module.exports = {
  title: 'Composition API & Options API',
  slug: 'composition-api',
  order: 2,
  topics: [
    {
      title: 'Options API vs Composition API',
      slug: 'options-vs-composition',
      order: 0,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>This is the defining question of Vue 3, and it is asked in almost every interview. The weak answer is "Composition API is newer, so it's better." The strong answer explains the specific problem the Options API could not solve — logic reuse and logic organisation at scale — and is honest that the Options API is still excellent for small components and is not deprecated.</p>`,
        `<p>It matters on the job because you will meet both. Vue 2 codebases being migrated, Vue 3 codebases written by teams who preferred the old style, and libraries documented in either — all normal. Being fluent in both, and able to say when each fits, is what "knows Vue" actually means.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>Both APIs build the same components with the same reactivity underneath. They differ in <strong>how you organise the code inside a component.</strong></p>`,
        `<p>The <strong>Options API</strong> organises by <em>option type</em>. All your state goes in <code>data</code>, all your derived values in <code>computed</code>, all your functions in <code>methods</code>, all your side effects in lifecycle hooks. Vue gives you the slots; you distribute your code among them.</p>`,
        `<p>The <strong>Composition API</strong> organises by <em>logical concern</em>. Everything about search — its state, its computed values, its functions, its watcher — sits together in one block of code, and can be lifted out into a function you import elsewhere.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch3-options-vs-composition.svg|Two component diagrams: the Options API version with one feature's code scattered across data, computed, methods and mounted; the Composition API version with each feature's code grouped in a contiguous block|The Options API fragments a feature across option types; the Composition API keeps each feature's code together.}}`,
        `<p>In a small component the difference is cosmetic. In a 400-line component with six features, the Options API forces you to scroll between four sections to understand any one of them — the "fragmentation problem." That is the real motivation, and it is what the interviewer wants you to name.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>The Composition API is a set of functions — <code>ref</code>, <code>computed</code>, <code>watch</code>, <code>onMounted</code> — called inside <code>setup()</code> (or, with <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code>, at the top level of the block). <code>setup()</code> runs once per component instance, before any lifecycle hook, and its job is to create the reactive state and behaviour the component needs.</p>`,
        `<p>There is one structural difference with far-reaching consequences: <strong>there is no <code>this</code>.</strong> In the Options API, Vue builds an instance object and binds <code>this</code> to it, which is why <code>data</code> must be a function and why arrow functions in <code>methods</code> break. In <code>setup()</code>, you hold your state in ordinary variables and closures. That is what makes logic <em>extractable</em>: a block of code that only refers to local variables can be cut, pasted into a function in another file, and imported — nothing binds it to a component instance any more.</p>`,
        `<p>That single property is the whole payoff, and it is why the Composition API beat the two Vue 2 reuse mechanisms:</p>`,
        `<table>`,
        `<thead><tr><th>Concern</th><th>Mixins (Vue 2)</th><th>Composition API</th></tr></thead>`,
        `<tbody>`,
        `<tr><td>Where does this property come from?</td><td>Unclear — merged implicitly from any mixin</td><td>Explicit — you see the import and the destructure</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Name collisions</td><td>Silent; last mixin wins</td><td>Impossible — you name the variables yourself</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Using the same logic twice</td><td>Cannot — one mixin merges once</td><td>Call the composable twice with different arguments</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>TypeScript</td><td>Poor — merged types are hard to infer</td><td>Excellent — plain functions with return types</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Passing arguments</td><td>Awkward</td><td>Normal function parameters</td></tr>`,
        `</tbody>`,
        `</table>`,
        `<p><strong>Better TypeScript</strong> is the other major win, and it is not a small one. The Options API requires Vue to infer types across a merged object of <code>data</code>, <code>computed</code>, <code>methods</code> and <code>props</code> reachable through <code>this</code> — solvable, but fragile. Composition API code is ordinary functions and variables, so inference is whatever TypeScript already does well.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>What the Options API keeps.</strong> It is genuinely more approachable: the structure is prescribed, so a beginner cannot organise a component badly, and code review is easier because everything is where you expect. Vue's team has stated it is not deprecated and will not be removed. Both styles are first-class in Vue 3 and can even coexist in one component during migration.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<p>The same component both ways. Options API first:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script&gt;\nexport default {\n  props: { userId: { type: Number, required: true } },\n\n  data() {\n    return {\n      user: null,\n      loading: false,\n      search: '',\n      results: [],\n    }\n  },\n\n  computed: {\n    displayName() { return this.user?.name ?? 'Anonymous' },\n    filtered()    { return this.results.filter(r =&gt; r.name.includes(this.search)) },\n  },\n\n  watch: {\n    userId: { handler: 'fetchUser', immediate: true },\n    search() { this.runSearch() },\n  },\n\n  methods: {\n    async fetchUser() {\n      this.loading = true\n      this.user = await api.getUser(this.userId)\n      this.loading = false\n    },\n    async runSearch() { this.results = await api.search(this.search) },\n  },\n\n  mounted() { this.runSearch() },\n}\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Note: to understand \"search\", you must read data, computed, watch,\n     methods AND mounted — four separate places. --&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Composition API — the same behaviour, grouped by feature:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref, computed, watch, onMounted } from 'vue'\n\nconst props = defineProps({ userId: { type: Number, required: true } })\n\n// ── Feature 1: the user ────────────────────────────────\nconst user = ref(null)\nconst loading = ref(false)\nconst displayName = computed(() =&gt; user.value?.name ?? 'Anonymous')\n\nasync function fetchUser() {\n  loading.value = true\n  user.value = await api.getUser(props.userId)\n  loading.value = false\n}\nwatch(() =&gt; props.userId, fetchUser, { immediate: true })\n\n// ── Feature 2: search ──────────────────────────────────\nconst search = ref('')\nconst results = ref([])\nconst filtered = computed(() =&gt; results.value.filter(r =&gt; r.name.includes(search.value)))\n\nasync function runSearch() { results.value = await api.search(search.value) }\nwatch(search, runSearch)\nonMounted(runSearch)\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>And the payoff — feature 2 lifts out into a file with no edits to its logic:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// composables/useSearch.js — now reusable in any component\nimport { ref, computed, watch, onMounted } from 'vue'\n\nexport function useSearch(searchFn) {\n  const search = ref('')\n  const results = ref([])\n  const filtered = computed(() =&gt;\n    results.value.filter(r =&gt; r.name.includes(search.value)),\n  )\n\n  async function run() { results.value = await searchFn(search.value) }\n\n  watch(search, run)\n  onMounted(run)\n\n  return { search, results, filtered, run }\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { useSearch } from '@/composables/useSearch'\n\n// Used twice in one component — impossible with a mixin\nconst users    = useSearch(api.searchUsers)\nconst products = useSearch(api.searchProducts)\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;input v-model="users.search"&gt;\n  &lt;input v-model="products.search"&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Why mixins were replaced, in one example:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ❌ Vue 2 mixins — where does \`loading\` come from? Which mixin won?\nexport default {\n  mixins: [fetchMixin, paginationMixin, sortMixin],\n  mounted() {\n    console.log(this.loading)   // declared in... one of the three? all three?\n  },\n}\n\n// ✅ Composables — the source of every binding is on the line that creates it\nconst { loading, data } = useFetch(url)\nconst { page, next } = usePagination()\nconst { sortKey } = useSort()\n// Collision? Rename it: const { loading: sortLoading } = useSort()</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Claiming the Options API is deprecated.</strong> It is not, and saying so in an interview signals you are repeating opinions rather than reading release notes.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using <code>this</code> in <code>setup()</code>.</strong> There is no instance yet — <code>this</code> is <code>undefined</code>. Props come from the <code>setup(props)</code> argument or <code>defineProps</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Thinking the Composition API is only about reuse.</strong> Organisation is at least as valuable: keeping a feature's code contiguous helps even in a component you never reuse.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Mixing both styles in one new component.</strong> Legal and occasionally useful during migration, confusing as a default.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming it is faster.</strong> The performance difference is negligible; <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> compiles slightly leaner output, but nobody should choose on that basis.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Reaching for <code>setup()</code> instead of <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code>.</strong> The explicit <code>setup()</code> function exists mainly for cases needing options alongside it; the sugar is the default.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Confusing "Composition API" with "<code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code>".</strong> The first is the set of functions; the second is compile-time sugar for using them. You can use the API without the sugar.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Use <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> with the Composition API for all new components — it is the officially recommended default.</li>`,
        `<li>Group code by feature and mark the groups with comments; that grouping is the point of the API.</li>`,
        `<li>Extract into a composable the moment a block of logic is needed twice, or the moment it is long enough to obscure the component.</li>`,
        `<li>Leave working Options API components alone. Rewrites for style alone cost regressions and buy nothing.</li>`,
        `<li>Use the Options API deliberately when it fits — small presentational components, or a team that is genuinely more productive with it.</li>`,
        `<li>If you are on TypeScript, the Composition API is the pragmatic choice; the inference story is far simpler.</li>`,
        `<li>Never introduce new mixins. Composables do the same job without the implicit merging.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Options API vs Composition API — which and why?"</strong><br>A: Same reactivity underneath; different code organisation. The Options API groups by option type — <code>data</code>, <code>computed</code>, <code>methods</code>, hooks — so a single feature's code is spread across four sections, and in a large component you scroll constantly. The Composition API groups by feature: state, computeds, functions and watchers for one concern sit together and can be extracted into a composable. I default to Composition with <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code>, especially with TypeScript, but the Options API is not deprecated and is a fine fit for small components.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What problem did the Composition API actually solve?"</strong><br>A: Two. Fragmentation — one feature's code scattered across option types, which gets painful past a few hundred lines. And logic reuse — Vue 2's options were mixins and scoped slots, and mixins have implicit sources, silent name collisions, no way to use the same logic twice in one component, and poor TypeScript. Composables are plain functions: explicit imports, no collisions because you name the bindings, reusable any number of times, and normal type inference.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why does the Composition API make logic reusable when mixins didn't?"</strong><br>A: Because there is no <code>this</code>. Options API code depends on the component instance, so it can't leave the component. Composition code holds state in ordinary variables and closures, so a block that only references its own locals can be moved into a function in another file unchanged. Reuse then becomes a plain function call — with arguments, a return value and, unlike a mixin, an obvious source for every binding.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Is the Options API going away?"</strong><br>A: No. Vue's team has been explicit that it stays, and both styles are first-class in Vue 3 — they can even coexist in one component, which matters for incremental migration. The Options API is more prescriptive, which makes it approachable and keeps codebases uniform; the Composition API scales better and types better. It is a choice, not a migration mandate.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Is the Composition API the same thing as <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code>?" (trap)</strong><br>A: No. The Composition API is the set of functions — <code>ref</code>, <code>computed</code>, <code>watch</code>, the lifecycle hooks — usable inside a <code>setup()</code> function. <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> is compile-time sugar that turns the whole block into that setup function and exposes top-level bindings to the template automatically. You can use the API without the sugar; you can't use the sugar without the API.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Same reactivity, different organisation: Options groups by <strong>option type</strong>, Composition by <strong>feature</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li>Problems solved: <strong>fragmentation</strong> + <strong>logic reuse</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li>No <code>this</code> in <code>setup()</code> → closures → logic is extractable → composables.</li>`,
        `<li>Mixins: implicit sources, silent collisions, single use, weak types. Composables: none of that.</li>`,
        `<li>TypeScript inference is far simpler with Composition.</li>`,
        `<li>Options API is <strong>not deprecated</strong>; both are first-class and can coexist.</li>`,
        `<li>Composition API ≠ <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> — the latter is sugar over the former.</li>`,
        `<li>Default for new code: <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> + Composition.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'setup(), script setup and the Compiler Macros',
      slug: 'script-setup',
      order: 1,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p><code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> is how virtually all modern Vue is written, so interviewers expect you to know not just how to use it but what it compiles to. The questions that follow — "why is there no <code>return</code>?", "why can't you import <code>defineProps</code>?", "why is a <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> component closed to its parent?" — all have the same root answer, and giving that answer once explains all three.</p>`,
        `<p>There is also a practical payoff. Knowing that the macros are compile-time constructs tells you immediately why you cannot call <code>defineProps</code> conditionally, why it cannot reference a variable declared in the same block, and why it produces such good TypeScript types. These are exactly the errors people hit in their first week.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p><code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> is <strong>syntactic sugar, resolved at build time</strong>, over the <code>setup()</code> function. The compiler takes the block's contents, makes them the body of a <code>setup()</code>, and arranges for every top-level binding — variables, functions, imports — to be visible to the template.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- What you write --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref } from 'vue'\nconst count = ref(0)\nfunction inc() { count.value++ }\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Roughly what the compiler produces --&gt;\n&lt;script&gt;\nimport { ref } from 'vue'\nexport default {\n  setup() {\n    const count = ref(0)\n    function inc() { count.value++ }\n    return { count, inc }        // ← generated for you\n  },\n}\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Three consequences follow directly, and they are the answers to the three questions above: no manual <code>return</code> is needed; importing a component is enough to use it in the template; and less boilerplate overall.</p>`,
        `<p>The <strong>compiler macros</strong> — <code>defineProps</code>, <code>defineEmits</code>, <code>defineExpose</code>, <code>defineModel</code>, <code>defineOptions</code>, <code>defineSlots</code> — look like functions but are not. They are markers the compiler recognises and <em>replaces</em>; no such function exists at runtime, which is why you never import them.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>Because it is a build-time transform, the compiler needs to analyse the macros <em>statically</em>. That single fact explains every restriction:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Macros must appear at the <strong>top level</strong> of <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> — never inside an <code>if</code>, a function or a loop. The compiler must find them without executing anything.</li>`,
        `<li>Macro arguments cannot reference local variables in the same block. The props declaration is hoisted out of the setup body, so a variable declared beside it does not exist yet at that point. Import from another module instead.</li>`,
        `<li>The macros can be compiled away entirely, so they cost nothing at runtime.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `{{fig:ch3-script-setup-compilation.svg|Compilation diagram showing a script setup block transformed into a setup function with an auto-generated return, and defineProps and defineEmits macros lifted into the component options object|The macros are lifted out into component options at compile time; the rest of the block becomes the setup body with a generated return.}}`,
        `<p><strong>Closed by default.</strong> A <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> component does not expose its internals to a parent holding a template ref. That is a deliberate reversal of Vue 2, where a parent could read and write any child's data. To publish an API you call <code>defineExpose({ … })</code>, which forces the component to state what is public.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Top-level <code>await</code>.</strong> <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> may use <code>await</code> at the top level, which turns the component into an async component that must be wrapped in <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code>. There is a subtle rule attached: after an <code>await</code>, the "current instance" context is lost, so lifecycle hooks and <code>inject()</code> must be registered <em>before</em> the first <code>await</code>.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>When you still need a plain <code>&lt;script&gt;</code>.</strong> Some things must run once per module rather than once per instance, or must be declared as options: <code>inheritAttrs</code>, a custom component <code>name</code>, module-level constants, or named exports. A file may contain both blocks, and they compile together. (Since 3.3, <code>defineOptions()</code> covers most of these without a second block.)</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\n// 1. Imports are top-level bindings → usable in the template, no registration\nimport UserCard from './UserCard.vue'\nimport { formatDate } from '@/utils/date'\nimport { ref, computed } from 'vue'\n\n// 2. Props — object form\nconst props = defineProps({\n  userId: { type: Number, required: true },\n  compact: { type: Boolean, default: false },\n})\n\n// 3. Emits\nconst emit = defineEmits(['select', 'close'])\n\n// 4. Two-way binding (3.4+) — declares the prop AND the emit\nconst open = defineModel('open', { type: Boolean, default: false })\n\n// 5. Options that used to need a second &lt;script&gt; block (3.3+)\ndefineOptions({ inheritAttrs: false, name: 'UserPanel' })\n\n// 6. Everything below is ordinary JS, auto-exposed to the template\nconst query = ref('')\nconst title = computed(() =&gt; \`User #\${props.userId}\`)\nfunction close() { open.value = false; emit('close') }\n\n// 7. Publish an explicit public API — without this the parent's ref sees nothing\ndefineExpose({ close })\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;section v-if="open"&gt;\n    &lt;h2&gt;{{ title }}&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;UserCard :user-id="userId" @select="emit('select', $event)" /&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;{{ formatDate(Date.now()) }}&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;input v-model="query"&gt;\n    &lt;button @click="close"&gt;Close&lt;/button&gt;\n  &lt;/section&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The static-analysis restrictions, made concrete:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ❌ Not top-level — the compiler can't find it without running the code\nif (someCondition) {\n  defineProps({ x: String })\n}\n\n// ❌ References a local: props are hoisted above this line, so \`types\` isn't defined yet\nconst types = { x: String }\ndefineProps(types)\n\n// ✅ Import it — module scope is available to the hoisted declaration\nimport { propTypes } from './props'\ndefineProps(propTypes)\n\n// ❌ Pointless and, historically, a warning — the macro is compiled away\nimport { defineProps } from 'vue'\n\n// ❌ There is no \`this\` in script setup\nfunction handler() { this.count++ }</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Both blocks together, for the cases the sugar cannot express:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script&gt;\n// Runs ONCE per module, not per instance\nconst REGISTRY = new Map()\n\nexport function registerWidget(id, fn) { REGISTRY.set(id, fn) }  // named export\n\nexport default { inheritAttrs: false }\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;script setup&gt;\n// Runs per component instance\nimport { onMounted } from 'vue'\nconst props = defineProps({ id: String })\nonMounted(() =&gt; REGISTRY.get(props.id)?.())\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Top-level <code>await</code>, and the ordering rule that catches people:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { onMounted, inject } from 'vue'\n\n// ✅ Register hooks and injections BEFORE the first await —\n//    after it, the current-instance context is gone.\nconst api = inject('api')\nonMounted(() =&gt; console.log('mounted'))\n\nconst user = await api.getUser()   // component becomes async\n\n// ❌ Too late — this hook is never registered\nonMounted(() =&gt; console.log('never runs'))\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Parent must wrap it --&gt;\n&lt;Suspense&gt;\n  &lt;AsyncUserPanel /&gt;\n  &lt;template #fallback&gt;Loading…&lt;/template&gt;\n&lt;/Suspense&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Importing the macros.</strong> They are compiled away; the import is dead weight and was previously a warning.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Calling a macro conditionally or inside a function.</strong> The compiler must find it statically at the top level.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Passing a locally declared object to <code>defineProps</code>.</strong> The declaration is hoisted above your variable. Import it instead.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting a parent ref to reach into the component.</strong> <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> is closed; use <code>defineExpose</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using <code>this</code>.</strong> There is no instance in setup. Everything is a local variable.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Registering hooks after a top-level <code>await</code>.</strong> They are silently ignored, because the current-instance context is lost across the await boundary.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> around a component with top-level <code>await</code>.</strong> Vue warns and the component does not render.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming a variable declared inside a function is available to the template.</strong> Only <em>top-level</em> bindings are exposed.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Use <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> for every new component; reach for a plain <code>setup()</code> only when you genuinely need it.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefer the TypeScript generic forms — <code>defineProps&lt;T&gt;()</code>, <code>defineEmits&lt;T&gt;()</code> — for real compile-time checking rather than development-only warnings.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>defineOptions()</code> (3.3+) instead of adding a second <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block just to set <code>inheritAttrs</code> or <code>name</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Expose the minimum with <code>defineExpose</code> — methods rather than raw state — so parents cannot depend on internals.</li>`,
        `<li>Order the block predictably: imports, macros, state, computeds, functions, watchers, lifecycle hooks. Consistency makes review fast.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep top-level <code>await</code> for the specific case where <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> is the intended UX; otherwise fetch in <code>onMounted</code> with an explicit loading state.</li>`,
        `<li>Install the Vue - Official (Volar) extension — most macro misuse is flagged as you type.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What does <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> compile to?"</strong><br>A: A <code>setup()</code> function whose body is the block's contents, plus a generated return exposing every top-level binding to the template. That is why there is no manual return and why importing a component is enough to use it. The macros are lifted out into the component's options object, so <code>defineProps</code> becomes the <code>props</code> option and <code>defineEmits</code> becomes <code>emits</code>. The compiler can also reference bindings directly in the render function instead of going through a returned object, which makes the output slightly leaner.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why can't you import <code>defineProps</code>?"</strong><br>A: Because it is not a function. It is a compiler macro — a marker the SFC compiler recognises and replaces at build time with a <code>props</code> option. Nothing exists at runtime to import. Same for <code>defineEmits</code>, <code>defineExpose</code>, <code>defineModel</code> and <code>defineOptions</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why must macros be at the top level, and why can't <code>defineProps</code> take a local variable?"</strong><br>A: Both fall out of static analysis. The compiler has to find and process the macros without executing the code, so they cannot be inside a condition, loop or function. And the props declaration is hoisted out of the setup body into the component options, so a variable declared next to it in the block does not exist at that point — you have to import the object from another module.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "A parent has a ref to my component but sees nothing on it. Why?" (trap)</strong><br>A: <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> components are closed by default — internals are not exposed to template refs. Call <code>defineExpose({ open, close })</code> to publish an explicit public API. It is deliberate: Vue 2 let a parent reach into any child's data, which made encapsulation impossible, so Vue 3 flipped the default.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "When do you still need a normal <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block?"</strong><br>A: When something must run once per module rather than per instance — module-level constants or a registry — or when you need named exports from the file. Historically also for options like <code>inheritAttrs</code> and <code>name</code>, but <code>defineOptions()</code> in 3.3+ covers those. Both blocks can coexist in one file.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What happens with top-level <code>await</code>?"</strong><br>A: The component becomes async and must be rendered inside <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code>. The catch is that the current-instance context is lost across the await, so <code>onMounted</code>, other lifecycle hooks and <code>inject()</code> must all be called before the first <code>await</code> — anything registered after it is silently ignored.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> = compile-time sugar over <code>setup()</code>; top-level bindings auto-exposed, no <code>return</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Imports are bindings ⇒ importing a component registers it.</li>`,
        `<li>Macros: <code>defineProps</code> · <code>defineEmits</code> · <code>defineExpose</code> · <code>defineModel</code> (3.4) · <code>defineOptions</code> (3.3) · <code>defineSlots</code>. <strong>Never imported.</strong></li>`,
        `<li>Macros must be <strong>top-level</strong> and can't reference locals — they're hoisted and statically analysed.</li>`,
        `<li>Components are <strong>closed by default</strong> → <code>defineExpose()</code> to publish an API.</li>`,
        `<li>No <code>this</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Top-level <code>await</code> ⇒ async component ⇒ needs <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code>; register hooks/inject <strong>before</strong> the await.</li>`,
        `<li>Plain <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> alongside it for module-scope code and named exports.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Composables: Reusable Stateful Logic',
      slug: 'composables',
      order: 2,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Composables are the reason the Composition API exists. Everything else — no <code>this</code>, functions instead of options, refs instead of <code>data</code> — is in service of making stateful logic extractable. So "write me a composable" is one of the most common practical Vue interview tasks, and "what makes a good composable?" one of the most common discussion questions.</p>`,
        `<p>They matter more than they first appear because they are how a Vue codebase stays maintainable past a certain size. Data fetching, form validation, pagination, keyboard shortcuts, local-storage persistence, media queries — every one of these is logic that a dozen components need and none of them should own. Without composables that logic gets copy-pasted; with them it has one home and one test suite.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>A <strong>composable</strong> is a plain function that uses Composition API functions to encapsulate reactive state and behaviour, and returns them for a component to use. By convention its name starts with <code>use</code>.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// The whole idea in eight lines\nimport { ref } from 'vue'\n\nexport function useCounter(initial = 0) {\n  const count = ref(initial)\n  const increment = () =&gt; count.value++\n  const reset = () =&gt; { count.value = initial }\n  return { count, increment, reset }\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The critical property, and the one interviewers probe: <strong>each call creates fresh state.</strong> Calling <code>useCounter()</code> twice gives two independent counters, because the refs are created inside the function body. That is what makes composables safely reusable — and what distinguishes them from a store, where the whole point is that everyone shares one instance.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch3-composable-instances.svg|Diagram contrasting a composable called in three components each getting its own isolated state, with a Pinia store shared as a single instance across the same three components|A composable creates new state per call; a store deliberately shares one instance. That distinction decides which you need.}}`,
        `<p>Composables can call other composables, which is where the name comes from — they compose. A <code>useUserProfile()</code> can be built from <code>useFetch()</code> plus <code>useLocalStorage()</code> without either knowing about the other.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>A composable is just a function; there is no registration and no framework magic. But because it calls Composition API functions, it inherits their constraints — and those constraints are the source of every composable bug.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>The setup-context rule.</strong> Lifecycle hooks (<code>onMounted</code>) and <code>inject()</code> resolve against the "currently active component instance," which Vue only sets while <code>setup()</code> runs. So a composable that registers hooks must be called <strong>synchronously during setup</strong> — not in an event handler, not after an <code>await</code>, not inside <code>onMounted</code> itself. Called correctly, its <code>onUnmounted</code> is bound to the calling component and cleanup happens automatically when that component goes away. That automatic cleanup is the single biggest practical advantage over a hand-rolled utility module.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Returning refs, not values.</strong> A composable must return reactive references. Returning <code>{ count: count.value }</code> hands back a frozen number. Return the refs themselves so consumers can destructure — refs survive destructuring, which is exactly why the convention is to return an object of refs rather than a <code>reactive</code> object.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Accepting refs as arguments.</strong> A composable that takes a URL should accept a ref, a getter or a plain value and normalise internally with <code>toValue()</code> (3.3+; previously <code>unref</code>). That way <code>useFetch(url)</code> works whether the caller passes a constant or a reactive value that should trigger a refetch.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Composable vs store.</strong> The dividing line is state ownership. Independent state per consumer → composable. One shared instance the whole app reads → Pinia. A composable that assigns its state at <em>module scope</em> rather than inside the function becomes a de facto singleton — that works, and is sometimes deliberate, but it is a store without the devtools, and on the server it leaks state between requests, which is a real bug in SSR.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<p>The canonical data-fetching composable, with reactive arguments and cleanup:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// composables/useFetch.js\nimport { ref, watchEffect, toValue, onUnmounted } from 'vue'\n\nexport function useFetch(url) {\n  const data = ref(null)\n  const error = ref(null)\n  const loading = ref(false)\n  let controller = null\n\n  async function run() {\n    controller?.abort()              // cancel any in-flight request\n    controller = new AbortController()\n\n    data.value = null\n    error.value = null\n    loading.value = true\n\n    try {\n      const res = await fetch(toValue(url), { signal: controller.signal })\n      if (!res.ok) throw new Error(\`HTTP \${res.status}\`)\n      data.value = await res.json()\n    } catch (e) {\n      if (e.name !== 'AbortError') error.value = e\n    } finally {\n      loading.value = false\n    }\n  }\n\n  // toValue() inside watchEffect means a ref/getter url is tracked and refetched\n  watchEffect(run)\n\n  // bound to the CALLING component — no cleanup code in the consumer\n  onUnmounted(() =&gt; controller?.abort())\n\n  return { data, error, loading, refetch: run }\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { computed } from 'vue'\nimport { useFetch } from '@/composables/useFetch'\n\nconst props = defineProps({ userId: Number })\n\n// a getter argument → refetches automatically when userId changes\nconst { data: user, loading, error } = useFetch(() =&gt; \`/api/users/\${props.userId}\`)\n\n// used twice, independently — impossible with a mixin\nconst { data: posts } = useFetch(() =&gt; \`/api/users/\${props.userId}/posts\`)\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;p v-if="loading"&gt;Loading…&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;p v-else-if="error"&gt;{{ error.message }}&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;UserCard v-else :user="user" :posts="posts" /&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Automatic cleanup — the pattern that makes composables worth using at all:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// composables/useEventListener.js\nimport { onMounted, onUnmounted } from 'vue'\n\nexport function useEventListener(target, event, handler, options) {\n  onMounted(() =&gt; target.addEventListener(event, handler, options))\n  onUnmounted(() =&gt; target.removeEventListener(event, handler, options))\n}\n\n// composables/useMouse.js — built FROM the one above\nimport { ref } from 'vue'\nimport { useEventListener } from './useEventListener'\n\nexport function useMouse() {\n  const x = ref(0)\n  const y = ref(0)\n  useEventListener(window, 'mousemove', (e) =&gt; { x.value = e.pageX; y.value = e.pageY })\n  return { x, y }        // consumers never think about removeEventListener\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// composables/useLocalStorage.js — reactive state synced to storage\nimport { ref, watch } from 'vue'\n\nexport function useLocalStorage(key, defaultValue) {\n  const stored = localStorage.getItem(key)\n  const value = ref(stored ? JSON.parse(stored) : defaultValue)\n\n  watch(value, (v) =&gt; {\n    if (v === null || v === undefined) localStorage.removeItem(key)\n    else localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(v))\n  }, { deep: true })\n\n  return value           // a single ref — return it directly, not wrapped\n}\n\n// Usage: const theme = useLocalStorage('theme', 'light')</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The three mistakes that break composables:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ❌ 1. Returning values instead of refs — consumer gets a frozen snapshot\nexport function useBad() {\n  const count = ref(0)\n  return { count: count.value }\n}\n\n// ❌ 2. Returning a reactive object — dies when the consumer destructures\nexport function useAlsoBad() {\n  const state = reactive({ count: 0 })\n  return state\n}\n// const { count } = useAlsoBad()   ← reactivity lost\n\n// ✅ Return an object of refs; destructuring is safe\nexport function useGood() {\n  const count = ref(0)\n  return { count, increment: () =&gt; count.value++ }\n}\n\n// ❌ 3. Called outside setup — onMounted has no instance to attach to\nfunction onClick() {\n  const { x } = useMouse()   // warns: \"onMounted called when there is no active component\"\n}\n\n// ⚠️ Module-scope state = a singleton, not per-consumer state.\n// Deliberate for a global toggle; an SSR cross-request leak if accidental.\nconst sharedCount = ref(0)\nexport function useSharedCounter() {\n  return { sharedCount }\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Returning <code>.value</code> instead of the ref.</strong> The consumer gets a number that never changes. Return refs.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Returning a <code>reactive</code> object.</strong> Consumers will destructure and lose reactivity. Return an object of refs, or a <code>reactive</code> object wrapped in <code>toRefs()</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Calling a composable outside setup.</strong> In an event handler or after an <code>await</code>, hooks and injections have no instance to bind to. Call it at the top of setup.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Accidental module-scope state.</strong> A ref declared outside the function is shared by every consumer — and on the server, by every request. Only do this on purpose.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting cleanup.</strong> Listeners, intervals and observers must be released in <code>onUnmounted</code>, or the composable leaks every time a component using it unmounts.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Accepting only plain values.</strong> A composable that cannot take a ref or getter forces callers to add their own watcher. Normalise with <code>toValue()</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Building a god-composable.</strong> <code>useEverything()</code> returning thirty bindings is a component in disguise. One concern per composable.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Reaching for a composable where a pure function would do.</strong> If there is no reactive state and no lifecycle, it is a utility — put it in <code>utils/</code> and skip the <code>use</code> prefix.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Name with the <code>use</code> prefix and one clear noun: <code>useAuth</code>, <code>usePagination</code>, <code>useIntersectionObserver</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Return an object of refs and functions so consumers can destructure and rename freely.</li>`,
        `<li>Accept refs, getters or plain values as arguments and normalise with <code>toValue()</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Register cleanup inside the composable with <code>onUnmounted</code> — the consumer should never have to remember it.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep each composable to one concern, and compose bigger ones from smaller ones.</li>`,
        `<li>Call composables synchronously at the top of <code>setup</code>, in a consistent order.</li>`,
        `<li>Test them as plain functions. Anything using lifecycle hooks needs a component wrapper (Vue Test Utils' <code>withSetup</code> helper pattern); anything that does not is testable directly.</li>`,
        `<li>Check VueUse before writing your own — it has around 200 well-tested composables, and reimplementing <code>useDebounce</code> is not a good use of a sprint.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What is a composable?"</strong><br>A: A plain function, conventionally named <code>useX</code>, that encapsulates reactive state and behaviour using Composition API functions and returns refs and methods for a component to consume. It is Vue's answer to stateful logic reuse — the replacement for mixins. Each call creates fresh state, so two components using the same composable get isolated instances, and any lifecycle hooks it registers are bound to the calling component so cleanup is automatic.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Composable or Pinia store?"</strong><br>A: It comes down to whether state should be shared. A composable creates new state per call — right for per-component concerns like a fetch, a form or a pagination cursor. A store is a single shared instance — right for auth, cart or notifications, where every component must see the same data. You <em>can</em> fake a store by declaring state at module scope in a composable, but then you lose devtools, SSR safety and HMR, so if you want a singleton, use Pinia.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why must composables be called during setup?"</strong><br>A: Because <code>onMounted</code>, <code>onUnmounted</code> and <code>inject</code> resolve against the currently active component instance, which Vue only sets while setup runs. Called from an event handler or after an <code>await</code>, there is no instance, so hooks are never registered and injections come back undefined. The upside of that same mechanism is automatic cleanup: hooks registered inside a composable belong to the calling component and fire when it unmounts.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why return refs rather than a reactive object?"</strong><br>A: Because consumers destructure. Destructuring a <code>reactive</code> object copies primitives out of the proxy and silently kills reactivity, whereas destructuring an object of refs copies the ref objects themselves and <code>.value</code> still routes through the getter. If a composable genuinely wants to hold a reactive object internally, it should return <code>toRefs(state)</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Composables vs mixins?"</strong><br>A: Mixins merge into the component implicitly, so you cannot tell where a property came from, names collide silently with last-one-wins, you cannot use the same mixin twice in one component, and types are hard to infer. A composable is an explicit function call: the import shows the source, you name the bindings so collisions are impossible, you can call it as many times as you like with different arguments, and it types like any other function.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Write a <code>useFetch</code> that refetches when its URL changes."</strong><br>A: Accept the URL as a ref, getter or string; hold <code>data</code>, <code>error</code> and <code>loading</code> refs; read the URL through <code>toValue()</code> inside a <code>watchEffect</code> so the dependency is tracked and the request re-runs on change; abort the previous request with an <code>AbortController</code> so an older response cannot overwrite a newer one; and abort again in <code>onUnmounted</code>. Return the three refs plus a manual <code>refetch</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Composable = plain <code>useX()</code> function encapsulating reactive state + behaviour.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Fresh state per call</strong> — that's what separates it from a store.</li>`,
        `<li>Return an <strong>object of refs</strong> (destructuring-safe), never <code>.value</code> and never a bare <code>reactive</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Call <strong>synchronously in setup</strong> — hooks and <code>inject</code> need the active instance.</li>`,
        `<li>Register cleanup with <code>onUnmounted</code> inside the composable → automatic for consumers.</li>`,
        `<li>Accept ref | getter | value; normalise with <code>toValue()</code> (3.3+).</li>`,
        `<li>Module-scope state ⇒ singleton ⇒ SSR cross-request leak. Use Pinia instead.</li>`,
        `<li>Composables replace mixins: explicit source, no collisions, reusable N times, typed.</li>`,
        `<li>No reactive state and no lifecycle? It's a util, not a composable.</li>`,
        `<li>Check <strong>VueUse</strong> first.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Lifecycle Hooks',
      slug: 'lifecycle-hooks',
      order: 3,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Lifecycle hooks are where a component touches the outside world: fetching data, measuring the DOM, starting timers, subscribing to sockets, and — crucially — undoing all of that. Interviewers ask about them to test two things: whether you know <em>when the DOM is actually available</em>, and whether you clean up after yourself. The second is where most candidates lose points, because a missing <code>onUnmounted</code> never shows up in development and always shows up in production as a slow memory leak.</p>`,
        `<p>The ordering questions matter too. "Parent or child mounted first?" sounds like trivia, but it is the difference between a component that works and one that reads <code>null</code> from a ref because it looked too early.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>A component passes through a fixed sequence of stages — created, mounted to the DOM, updated on state changes, and finally unmounted. Vue lets you register a function to run at each stage.</p>`,
        `<p>In the Composition API, hooks are functions you call inside <code>setup</code>:</p>`,
        `<table>`,
        `<thead><tr><th>Composition API</th><th>Options API</th><th>Fires when</th><th>DOM ready?</th></tr></thead>`,
        `<tbody>`,
        `<tr><td><em>(setup body)</em></td><td><code>beforeCreate</code> / <code>created</code></td><td>Instance created, reactivity live</td><td>❌</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>onBeforeMount</code></td><td><code>beforeMount</code></td><td>Just before first render is inserted</td><td>❌</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>onMounted</code></td><td><code>mounted</code></td><td>Component is in the DOM</td><td>✅</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>onBeforeUpdate</code></td><td><code>beforeUpdate</code></td><td>State changed, DOM not yet patched</td><td>✅ (old)</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>onUpdated</code></td><td><code>updated</code></td><td>DOM patched after a change</td><td>✅ (new)</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>onBeforeUnmount</code></td><td><code>beforeUnmount</code></td><td>About to be removed; still fully usable</td><td>✅</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>onUnmounted</code></td><td><code>unmounted</code></td><td>Removed; children torn down</td><td>❌</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>onErrorCaptured</code></td><td><code>errorCaptured</code></td><td>A descendant threw</td><td>—</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>onActivated</code> / <code>onDeactivated</code></td><td>same</td><td>KeepAlive cache in/out</td><td>✅</td></tr>`,
        `</tbody>`,
        `</table>`,
        `<p>The two that carry almost all the weight in practice are <strong><code>onMounted</code></strong> (the DOM exists — measure it, focus it, initialise a chart) and <strong><code>onUnmounted</code></strong> (release everything you claimed).</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch3-lifecycle.svg|Vue component lifecycle timeline showing setup, beforeMount, mounted, the update loop of beforeUpdate and updated, and beforeUnmount and unmounted, with parent-child ordering annotated|The lifecycle, with the ordering that matters: parents start first but children finish mounting first.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong><code>created</code> has no Composition API equivalent</strong> because the body of <code>setup()</code> <em>is</em> that moment. Code at the top of <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> runs when the instance is created and reactivity is live but nothing is rendered — exactly what <code>created</code> meant.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Parent/child ordering</strong> is the classic question, and the rule is: <em>parents start first, children finish first.</em> Mounting is depth-first — the parent begins mounting, which requires mounting its children, and a parent cannot be "in the DOM" until its subtree is. So:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-text">Parent setup → Parent beforeMount → Child setup → Child beforeMount\n            → Child mounted → Parent mounted</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Unmounting reverses: <code>Parent beforeUnmount</code> → <code>Child beforeUnmount</code> → <code>Child unmounted</code> → <code>Parent unmounted</code>. The practical consequence: in a parent's <code>onMounted</code>, all children are mounted and their refs are populated — which is why that is the right place to measure or focus.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Where should data fetching go?</strong> Two defensible answers. Fetching in the setup body starts the request marginally earlier, which matters on slow connections. Fetching in <code>onMounted</code> is the safer default and is required if the request touches the DOM. In SSR the difference is decisive: <code>onMounted</code> <strong>never runs on the server</strong>, so anything there is client-only — which is precisely why browser-only APIs (<code>window</code>, <code>IntersectionObserver</code>, <code>localStorage</code>) belong in <code>onMounted</code> and nowhere else.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>onUpdated</code> is a trap.</strong> Changing reactive state inside it triggers another update, which fires <code>onUpdated</code> again — an infinite loop. It also fires for <em>any</em> change in the component, so it is a blunt instrument; a <code>watch</code> on the specific value is almost always what you actually wanted.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>onErrorCaptured</code></strong> catches errors from descendants and can stop propagation by returning <code>false</code>. It is how you build an error-boundary component. Note it does not catch errors in its own component, and async errors only if they are awaited within a captured call.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code> changes everything.</strong> A cached component is not unmounted when it disappears — it is deactivated. <code>onUnmounted</code> stops firing on each switch, so per-visit work must move to <code>onActivated</code>/<code>onDeactivated</code>. A timer stopped in <code>onUnmounted</code> under a <code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code> simply never stops.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport {\n  onBeforeMount, onMounted, onBeforeUpdate, onUpdated,\n  onBeforeUnmount, onUnmounted, onErrorCaptured, ref,\n} from 'vue'\n\nconst chartEl = ref(null)\nlet chart = null\nlet timer = null\nlet observer = null\n\n// setup body ≙ created: reactivity live, no DOM yet\nconsole.log('created — no DOM')\n\nonBeforeMount(() =&gt; console.log('about to insert into the DOM'))\n\nonMounted(() =&gt; {\n  // ✅ The DOM exists. Refs are populated. Browser APIs are safe.\n  chart = new Chart(chartEl.value, chartConfig)\n  timer = setInterval(poll, 5000)\n  observer = new IntersectionObserver(onIntersect)\n  observer.observe(chartEl.value)\n  window.addEventListener('resize', onResize)\n})\n\nonBeforeUpdate(() =&gt; { /* old DOM still in place — capture scroll position here */ })\nonUpdated(() =&gt; { /* ⚠️ never set reactive state here — infinite loop */ })\n\nonBeforeUnmount(() =&gt; {\n  // still fully functional — good place to persist state\n  localStorage.setItem('scroll', chartEl.value.scrollTop)\n})\n\nonUnmounted(() =&gt; {\n  // ✅ Release EVERYTHING claimed in onMounted\n  clearInterval(timer)\n  observer?.disconnect()\n  window.removeEventListener('resize', onResize)\n  chart?.destroy()\n})\n\nonErrorCaptured((err, instance, info) =&gt; {\n  report(err, info)\n  return false            // stop the error propagating further up\n})\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;&lt;canvas ref="chartEl" /&gt;&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Ordering, demonstrated — worth running once to make it stick:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// Parent.vue                    // Child.vue\nonMounted(() =&gt; log('P mount'))  onMounted(() =&gt; log('C mount'))\nonUnmounted(() =&gt; log('P un'))   onUnmounted(() =&gt; log('C un'))\n\n// Mount order:    C mount → P mount   (children complete first)\n// Unmount order:  C un    → P un      (children torn down first)\n//\n// So a parent's onMounted is the first safe place to touch child refs.</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Hooks inside composables — the cleanup pattern that makes them worth using:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// The consumer writes ONE line and gets correct cleanup for free.\nexport function useInterval(fn, ms) {\n  let id = null\n  onMounted(() =&gt; { id = setInterval(fn, ms) })\n  onUnmounted(() =&gt; clearInterval(id))\n}\n\n// In a component:  useInterval(poll, 5000)</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The <code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code> trap:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { onMounted, onUnmounted, onActivated, onDeactivated } from 'vue'\n\n// ❌ Under &lt;KeepAlive&gt; these fire ONCE — the timer never stops on tab switch\nonMounted(() =&gt; startPolling())\nonUnmounted(() =&gt; stopPolling())\n\n// ✅ Fire on every show/hide of a cached component\nonActivated(() =&gt; startPolling())\nonDeactivated(() =&gt; stopPolling())\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Touching the DOM before <code>onMounted</code>.</strong> Template refs are <code>null</code> during setup. Any <code>querySelector</code>, focus call or measurement belongs in <code>onMounted</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>No cleanup in <code>onUnmounted</code>.</strong> Intervals, listeners, observers, WebSockets and third-party instances all outlive the component otherwise. This is the most common real leak in Vue apps.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Mutating state in <code>onUpdated</code>.</strong> Infinite update loop. Use a <code>watch</code> on the specific value instead.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using browser APIs outside <code>onMounted</code> in an SSR app.</strong> <code>window is not defined</code> at build or request time. <code>onMounted</code> never runs on the server, which is exactly what makes it the safe place.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Registering hooks asynchronously.</strong> A hook called inside a <code>setTimeout</code>, a promise callback or after a top-level <code>await</code> has no active instance and is silently ignored.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting <code>onUnmounted</code> under <code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code>.</strong> Cached components deactivate rather than unmount; use <code>onActivated</code>/<code>onDeactivated</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Looking for <code>onCreated</code>.</strong> It does not exist — the setup body is that moment.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming <code>onMounted</code> means "children are rendered and measured".</strong> They are mounted, but if a child renders asynchronously (<code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code>, async component), its content may not be there yet.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Pair every acquisition in <code>onMounted</code> with a release in <code>onUnmounted</code>, written at the same time.</li>`,
        `<li>Better still, wrap the pair in a composable so consumers cannot forget — that is what composables are for.</li>`,
        `<li>Put all browser-only code in <code>onMounted</code>; it is the SSR-safe boundary.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefer <code>watch</code>/<code>watchEffect</code> over <code>onUpdated</code> when you care about a specific value — narrower, cheaper and loop-free.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep hooks thin: call a named function rather than inlining thirty lines, so the sequence stays readable.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>onErrorCaptured</code> to build a real error boundary around risky subtrees, and report to your monitoring service there and in <code>app.config.errorHandler</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Register hooks synchronously at the top level of setup, never conditionally.</li>`,
        `<li>Remember <code>await nextTick()</code> when you need the DOM to reflect a change you just made — hooks tell you about lifecycle stages, <code>nextTick</code> about the render queue.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Walk me through the lifecycle hooks and what you'd use each for."</strong><br>A: The setup body replaces <code>created</code> — reactivity is live, no DOM. <code>onBeforeMount</code> is rarely needed. <code>onMounted</code> is where the DOM exists: refs are populated, so measure, focus, initialise third-party libraries, start timers, use browser APIs. <code>onBeforeUpdate</code>/<code>onUpdated</code> bracket a re-render — useful for capturing scroll position before and restoring after, but a watcher is usually better. <code>onBeforeUnmount</code> still has a working component, so persist state there; <code>onUnmounted</code> is for releasing everything. <code>onErrorCaptured</code> builds error boundaries, and <code>onActivated</code>/<code>onDeactivated</code> replace mount/unmount under <code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Parent or child <code>mounted</code> first?"</strong><br>A: Child. Mounting is depth-first: the parent starts mounting, which requires mounting its children, and the parent cannot be considered in the DOM until its subtree is. So the order is parent setup, parent beforeMount, child setup, child beforeMount, child mounted, parent mounted. Unmounting mirrors it — children are torn down before the parent. The practical consequence is that a parent's <code>onMounted</code> is the first place its children's refs are safe to read.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Where do you fetch data, and why?"</strong><br>A: Either the setup body or <code>onMounted</code>. The setup body fires the request slightly earlier, which helps on slow connections; <code>onMounted</code> is required if the response touches the DOM, and is the safe default. The deciding factor is SSR: <code>onMounted</code> never runs on the server, so putting a fetch there makes it client-only. If the data must be server-rendered, fetch in setup — with <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> or a framework-level data hook like Nuxt's <code>useAsyncData</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why is there no <code>onCreated</code>?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Because <code>setup()</code> itself runs at that point in the lifecycle. The instance exists, props are resolved and reactivity is live, but nothing has been rendered — precisely what <code>created</code> meant. So the top of <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> is the <code>created</code> hook; adding a separate function would be redundant. <code>beforeCreate</code> is gone for the same reason: setup runs before it would have.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What breaks when you wrap a component in <code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code>?"</strong><br>A: Anything relying on unmount. A cached component is deactivated, not destroyed, so <code>onUnmounted</code> stops firing on each switch — a timer or subscription cleaned up there will keep running forever. Move per-visit setup and teardown to <code>onActivated</code>/<code>onDeactivated</code>. <code>onMounted</code> also stops re-running, so per-visit refetching has to move too.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you prevent memory leaks in a Vue component?"</strong><br>A: Release in <code>onUnmounted</code> everything acquired in <code>onMounted</code>: <code>clearInterval</code>/<code>clearTimeout</code>, <code>removeEventListener</code>, <code>observer.disconnect()</code>, socket close, and <code>destroy()</code> on third-party instances. Watchers and computeds created during setup are stopped automatically; ones created manually outside a component scope are not. The most reliable approach is to wrap acquisition and release together in a composable so the consumer cannot forget half of it.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>setup body ≙ <code>created</code>. No <code>onCreated</code> exists.</li>`,
        `<li><code>onMounted</code> = DOM ready, refs populated, browser APIs safe, <strong>never runs on the server</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>onUnmounted</code> = release timers, listeners, observers, sockets, library instances.</li>`,
        `<li>Order: <strong>parent starts first, child finishes first</strong>; unmount reverses.</li>`,
        `<li>Never set reactive state in <code>onUpdated</code> — infinite loop. Use <code>watch</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code> ⇒ <code>onActivated</code>/<code>onDeactivated</code> replace mount/unmount.</li>`,
        `<li><code>onErrorCaptured</code> → error boundary; <code>return false</code> stops propagation.</li>`,
        `<li>Register hooks <strong>synchronously in setup</strong> — not after <code>await</code>, not in callbacks.</li>`,
        `<li>Wrap acquire+release pairs in a composable so cleanup can't be forgotten.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
  ],
}
